---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import MethodRail from '../components/MethodRail.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import BeforeAfter from '../components/BeforeAfter.astro';
import { galleryPairs } from '../data/gallery';
import { guides } from '../data/guides';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const pairs = ['fabric-sofa-2', 'stair-carpet-2'].
  map((id) => galleryPairs.find((p) => p.id === id)).
  filter((p): p is (typeof galleryPairs)[number] => Boolean(p));

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between steam cleaning and hot water extraction?',
    a: 'In practice they are the same thing, and "steam cleaning" is a slight misnomer. Hot water extraction sprays hot water and cleaning solution into the fibre under pressure and immediately vacuums it back out. It is not steam in the true sense — actual steam would be too hot for most upholstery.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When do you use a low-moisture method instead?',
    a: 'On velvet, viscose blends, silk, some linens, S-coded fabrics, and anything that fails a moisture test on a hidden seam. It costs the same. The choice is made after testing the fabric, not before.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you use chemicals?',
    a: 'Yes — cleaning is chemistry, and anyone claiming otherwise is selling something. What matters is which ones and whether they are rinsed out. We use pH-balanced, low-odour products chosen for the fibre, and the rinse step is the one that makes them safe around children and pets.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does the order of the steps matter so much?',
    a: 'Because most failures in this trade are sequencing failures. Wetting before vacuuming turns dry grit into mud. Extracting before the pre-spray has dwelled leaves the oily bond intact. Skipping the drying passes causes browning and slow drying. The individual techniques are not secret; doing all of them, in order, every time, is the job.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${site.domain}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'How we clean', item: `${site.domain}/how-we-clean/` },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': `${site.domain}/how-we-clean/#faq`,
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="How We Clean | Our 7-Step Upholstery & Carpet Method"
  description="The seven-step method we use on every job: fibre testing, dry soil removal, pre-spray and dwell, agitation, hot water extraction, spot work, and controlled drying."
  schema={schema}
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Method"
    title="How we actually clean things"
    lede="There is no secret product. There is a sequence, and the discipline to do all of it every time — which is the part that separates a result you notice from one you do not."
    trail={[{ href: '/how-we-clean/', label: 'How we clean' }]}
  />

  <section class="container-page py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 class="mt-0">Most of the dirt is dry</h2>
        <p>
          The single biggest misunderstanding about upholstery and carpet is that the problem is
          stains. It usually is not. The bulk of what comes out of a sofa or a carpet is dry
          particulate — skin, hair, fibre, city dust, garden grit — sitting at the base of the pile.
        </p>
        <p>
          That matters because dry soil is abrasive. Every time somebody sits down or walks across a
          room, grit grinds against fibre. A carpet does not just look dirty as it ages; it is being
          physically sanded from underneath, and that wear is permanent.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also dictates the method. Dry soil comes out with a vacuum. Introduce water first and
          you have made mud, and driven it deeper. This is why the vacuuming stage takes longer than
          people expect, and why skipping it is the most common corner cut in the trade.
        </p>

        <h2>The bound layer needs chemistry, not pressure</h2>
        <p>
          What is left after vacuuming is the oily layer: body oils, cooking residue, hair product,
          traffic film. That is genuinely bonded to the fibre, and no amount of scrubbing or water
          pressure will shift it directly.
        </p>
        <p>
          It has to be chemically released first, with an alkaline pre-spray that needs time to work
          — usually eight to ten minutes of dwell. Rushing that is why a hurried job looks acceptable
          while it is wet and disappointing once it dries.
        </p>

        <h2>Then take out more than you put in</h2>
        <p>
          Extraction is the part people picture, and it is the least complicated. Hot solution goes
          in under pressure, dirty solution comes straight back out. The skill is restraint: the
          target is the least moisture that will do the job, followed by dry passes that recover
          considerably more than went in.
        </p>
        <p>
          Over-wetting causes nearly every post-clean complaint we get called to fix — browning as
          soil wicks up from the foam, shrinkage on natural fibres, damp underlay, and a musty smell
          that appears two days later.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="grid gap-8">
          {
            pairs.map((p) => (
              <BeforeAfter
                id={p.id}
                label={p.label}
                caption={p.caption}
                beforeAlt={p.beforeAlt}
                afterAlt={p.afterAlt}
              />
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <MethodRail heading="The seven steps" lede="Every job, domestic or commercial, runs through all seven in this order." />

  <section class="bg-sand-100 py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container-page grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink-950">What we will not do</h2>
        <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          {
            [
              'Clean a fabric we cannot safely identify. We test first, and if the answer is no, we say so on the doorstep and do not charge for the visit.',
              'Promise a stain will disappear when we think it will only reduce. You will get the honest version before you book.',
              'Mask an odour with fragrance and call it odour removal. If the source cannot be reached, we will tell you what it would actually take.',
              'Use heat on a protein stain, or bleach on a coloured fabric, to get a quick result that damages the piece.',
              'Leave a job so wet that it is still damp the next day.',
            ].map((i) => (
              <li class="flex items-start gap-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-900">
                <svg class="mt-1 h-5 w-5 shrink-0 text-ink-600" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true">
                  <path d="M5 5l10 10M15 5 5 15" />
                </svg>
                {i}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink-950">Read more on the detail</h2>
        <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3">
          {
            guides.map((g) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={`/guides/${g.slug}/`}
                  class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4 rounded-2xl bg-sand-50 px-5 py-4 ring-1 ring-ink-950/10 hover:ring-ink-950/35"
                >
                  <span>
                    <span class="font-semibold text-ink-950">{g.nav}</span>
                    <span class="mt-0.5 block text-sm text-ink-700">{g.summary}</span>
                  </span>
                  <svg class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-ink-600" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true">
                    <path d="M3 8h10M9 4l4 4-4 4" />
                  </svg>
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq faqs={faqs} heading="Questions about the method" />

  <CtaBand />
</Base>
